        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.05,
            0.432,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 7 | Part-II"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.088,
            0.847,
            0.145
          ],
          "content": "Try again , and choose different numbers this time. What product did you get? Was it different from the first time? Try a few more times with different numbers!"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.146,
            0.796,
            0.167
          ],
          "content": "Play the same game with the grid below. What answer do you get?"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.17,
            0.186,
            0.394,
            0.354
          ],
          "content": "| 8 | -4 | 12 | -6 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| -28 | 14 | -42 | 21 |\n| 12 | -6 | 18 | -9 |\n| 20 | -10 | 30 | -15 |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_14_table_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.422,
            0.235,
            0.813,
            0.313
          ],
          "content": "What is so special about these grids? Is the magic in the numbers or the way they are arranged or both? Can you make more such grids?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.381,
            0.361,
            0.401
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Division of Integers"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.406,
            0.847,
            0.464
          ],
          "content": "We have earlier seen how division can be converted into multiplication. For example, (– 100) ÷ 25 can be reframed as, ‘what should be multiplied to 25 to get (– 100)?’. That is,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.423,
            0.469,
            0.574,
            0.487
          ],
          "content": "25 \\times 2 = (-100).",
          "image_source": "images/page_14_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.493,
            0.319,
            0.511
          ],
          "content": "We know that"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.402,
            0.512,
            0.589,
            0.531
          ],
          "content": "25 \\times (-4) = (-100).",
          "image_source": "images/page_14_equation_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.536,
            0.285,
            0.555
          ],
          "content": "Therefore,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.408,
            0.555,
            0.588,
            0.574
          ],
          "content": "(-100) \\div 25 = (-4).",
          "image_source": "images/page_14_equation_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.585,
            0.846,
            0.624
          ],
          "content": "Similarly, $(-100) \\div (-4)$ can be reframed as, ‘What should be multiplied to (– 4) to get (– 100)?’"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.414,
            0.624,
            0.581,
            0.642
          ],
          "content": "(-4) \\times ? = (-100).",
          "image_source": "images/page_14_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.648,
            0.319,
            0.667
          ],
          "content": "We know that"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.409,
            0.668,
            0.588,
            0.686
          ],
          "content": "( - 4 ) \\times 2 5 = ( - 1 0 0 ) .",
          "image_source": "images/page_14_equation_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.691,
            0.285,
            0.71
          ],
          "content": "Therefore,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.41,
            0.71,
            0.588,
            0.729
          ],
          "content": "(-100) \\div (-4) = 25.",
          "image_source": "images/page_14_equation_18.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.735,
            0.412,
            0.754
          ],
          "content": "Similarly, we know that"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.416,
            0.754,
            0.583,
            0.773
          ],
          "content": "( - 25 ) \\times ( - 2 ) = 50.",
          "image_source": "images/page_14_equation_20.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.778,
            0.285,
            0.797
          ],
          "content": "Therefore,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.415,
            0.797,
            0.583,
            0.816
          ],
          "content": "50 \\div (-25) = (-2).",
          "image_source": "images/page_14_equation_22.jpg"
        },
        {
